  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Monday: Fog Monday Morning - Dry Conditions\n\nVisible satellite imagery early this afternoon depicts extensive \ncloud cover across much of the Upper Midwest. Must of this cloud \ncover is mid to low level clouds, which will aid in keeping \nafternoon highs a few degrees cooler today. There are some lingering \nlight rain showers and/or sprinkles across portions of the area, but \nthat activity should continue to wane through the afternoon.\n\nHigh surface humidity along with surface high pressure and light \nwinds will contribute in the development of fog and continued low \nclouds into Monday morning. The latest 27/12z HREF and REFS guidance \nboth are showing high chances (60-80%) for visibility to drop down \nto 1 mile or less, with the higher potential across southeastern MN \nand west of the Mississippi River. Dense fog may even form as a \nresult for some locations, which will lead to adverse driving \nconditions to start the day on Monday. Any fog that does develop \nshould quickly dissipate after sunrise. Dry conditions are forecast \nthe rest of the day and temperatures should be able to rise into the \n70s for most locations with decreasing cloud cover into the \nafternoon.\n\nTuesday through Thursday: Rain Chances Return\n\nAn upper level low pressure system should be located over the Desert \nSouthwest in the middle of next week. This low will begin to become \ncutoff on Tuesday into Wednesday, which should slow down its \nprogression as it continues to move east and eventually northeast. \nMultiple sources of moisture, including the remnants of Hurricane \nPolo, will move in over the Plains into the Great Lakes region \nduring this time frame. There remains a fair amount of uncertainty \nas to where the heaviest rainfall totals will fall this far north, \nbut the consensus appears to be slowly leaning towards the higher \ntotals remaining just to the south of the local forecast area. Even \nwith that being said, higher totals could still reach into \nnortheastern IA to far southern WI, but those details will be \nrefined over the following few days. Ensemble forecast guidance \nsuggests mean PWAT values of around 1.5-1.75 inches are possible \nwhich could lead to moderate to heavy rainfall rates. If the \nheaviest rainfall bands can get this far north, 2-3+ inches of \nrainfall from this multiple day system cannot be ruled out (10% \nchance or lower). If most of that rainfall occurs in a short \nduration or over areas that have seen plentiful rain recently, \nflooding may result. \n\nFriday into the Weekend: Drier and Trending Cooler\n\nThe plume of deep moisture should get pushed off to the south \nThursday into Friday as troughing attempts to dig south over the \nGreat Lakes into the northeastern US. There is quite a large \ndiscrepancy on just how far south and how quickly the trough digs \nsouth out of Canada, which may have a pretty large impact on the \nweather going into next weekend. This feature will usher in a cold \nfront, which will lead to cooler temperatures, just how cool is the \nmain question. As of now, afternoon highs are forecast to be in the \nmid to lower 60s but overnight lows may drop into the lower 40s to \n30s if the cooler solutions pan out.",
